@@s001 Data as vectors
@@s002 dato:the components,ok:the vector
@@s003 ora:the row being read,dato:each person is a point
@@s004 dato:points of the pose,ora:point being copied,ok:the vector
@@s005 A simplified pose with 3 points (the real one has 25). Each point has two coordinates.
@@s006 The coordinates are copied one after the other: first x and y of P1…
@@s007 …then of P2…
@@s008 …then of P3. The pose has become <b>one vector</b> of 6 numbers, always in the same order: x1, y1, x2, y2, …
@@s009 With 25 points there is a vector of 50 numbers: one row of <code>poses.npy</code>, shape (18, 50). The fixed order is what allows two poses to be compared component by component.
@@s010 ora:row being copied,ok:the vector
@@s011 dato:u and v,ora:the operation,ok:the result
@@s012 dato:u and v,ora:one piece of the combination,ok:3u + 2v
@@s013 Two vectors: u = [1, 0] and v = [0, 1].
@@s014 u is multiplied by the weight 3: 3u = [3, 0], an arrow three times longer.
@@s015 v is multiplied by 2 and attached at the tail: 2v = [0, 2].
@@s016 The sum is the arrow from the origin to the end: 3u + 2v = <b>[3, 2]</b>. Weights for vectors, then sum: it is a linear combination, like w₁x₁ + w₂x₂.
@@s017 dato:the two poses,ora:the difference,ok:the distance
@@s018 Data as vectors
@@s019 The idea of week 2 (<i>Vector representations</i>): any datum can be written as a list of numbers, that is a vector. Here: what a vector is, how a table, a pose and an image become vectors, the two basic operations, the linear combination and the move to many dimensions.
@@s020 What a vector is
@@s021 A <b>vector</b> is an <b>ordered</b> list of numbers. Each number is called a <b>component</b>. In the slides it is written as a column (<i>column vector</i>), in the text often in square brackets: <span class="m">v = [3, 2]</span>. In NumPy it is a 1D array: <code>np.array([3, 2])</code>.
@@s022 The order matters: the first component always says the same thing (for example the height), the second another (the weight). Swapping them changes the meaning, so [3, 2] and [2, 3] are different vectors.
@@s023 A vector with 2 components can be drawn in two equivalent ways: as a <b>point</b> of the plane, or as an <b>arrow</b> that starts from the origin and reaches that point. The number of components is the <b>dimension</b>: it is written <span class="m">v ∈ ℝⁿ</span>, "v belongs to R-n", that is v is a list of n real numbers.
@@s024 Question asked in chat: what does Vector representations mean?
@@s025 Writing a datum (a pose, an image, a measurement) as a list of numbers. It is the bridge that allows using length, distance and inner product on things that are not arrows.
@@s026 v = [3, 2]: components, point, arrow
@@s027 Feature: each measurement is a component
@@s028 On slide 38 the question is: "which characteristics distinguish a person?" Height, weight, shoe size… Each one is a <b>feature</b> (measured characteristic). Putting the features of a person in a row gives their <b>feature vector</b> (<i>feature vector</i>).
@@s029 A table of data is therefore a stack of vectors: <b>one row per example</b>, <b>one column per feature</b>. With 2 features each example is a point of the plane; with 50 features a point of ℝ⁵⁰.
@@s030 The gain is huge: similar examples become <b>nearby</b> points. The question "how similar are two people?" becomes "how far apart are two points?", and distances can be computed. Watch the units: if one feature is in centimetres and the other in kilograms, the one with the larger numbers weighs more in the distance; that is why one normalises first (week 7).
@@s031 A table is a cloud of points
@@s032 How a pose becomes a vector
@@s033 The exercise <code>01-poses</code> uses badminton poses: a body described by <b>25 points</b> (head, shoulders, elbows…), each with an <span class="m">x</span> and a <span class="m">y</span>. To make a vector all the coordinates are put in a row, always in the same order: x₁, y₁, x₂, y₂, …, x₂₅, y₂₅. Result: a vector of <b>50 components</b>.
@@s034 The file <code>poses.npy</code> has shape <code>(18, 50)</code>: 18 poses, one per row, 50 numbers each. The fixed order is essential: component 3 must be the x of the second point in <b>all</b> the poses, otherwise comparing them component by component would make no sense.
@@s035 At the exam, in English
@@s036 "Each pose is represented as a <b>vector in R<sup>50</sup></b>, so the similarity between two poses is the <b>Euclidean distance</b> between their vectors."
@@s037 From points to a list of numbers
@@s038 Images as vectors
@@s039 A digital greyscale image is a grid of <b>pixels</b>, and each pixel is a number (usually from 0, black, to 255, white). A grid is a matrix, but for many models a vector is needed: the image is <b>flattened</b> (<i>flatten</i>) by putting the rows one after the other.
@@s040 An image of 28 × 28 pixels thus becomes a vector of 784 components, a point of ℝ⁷⁸⁴ (slide 40: "images as vectors, feature vectors"). In NumPy: <code>img.flatten()</code> or <code>img.reshape(-1)</code>. For colour images each pixel has 3 numbers (red, green, blue), and the vector becomes three times longer.
@@s041 Using the pixels directly as features is the simplest way; in weeks 8 and 11 better features computed from the pixels are seen (edges, histograms of gradients).
@@s042 A 3 × 3 cross becomes a vector of ℝ⁹
@@s043 Vector sum and scalar multiplication
@@s044 The two basic operations (slide 43) are done <b>component by component</b>.
@@s045 <b>Sum</b>: <span class="m">u + v = [u₁ + v₁, u₂ + v₂, …]</span>. Only vectors of the <b>same dimension</b> can be added. In the drawing, the arrow v is attached at the tail of arrow u: the sum goes from the origin to the end of the chain.
@@s046 <b>Scalar multiplication</b>: a <b>scalar</b> is a single number, c. <span class="m">c·u = [c·u₁, c·u₂, …]</span>. The arrow stays on the same line: it lengthens if |c| &gt; 1, shortens if |c| &lt; 1, and reverses direction if c is negative.
@@s047 From these two comes the <b>difference</b>: <span class="m">u − v = u + (−1)·v</span>, the arrow from the tip of v to the tip of u. It is the basis of the distance between two data.
@@s048 u + v, 2u and −v
@@s049 Linear combination
@@s050 A <b>linear combination</b> puts the two operations together: each vector is multiplied by a number (a <b>weight</b>, or coefficient) and the results are summed. With the vectors v₁, …, vₖ and the weights c₁, …, cₖ: <span class="m">c₁v₁ + c₂v₂ + … + cₖvₖ</span>.
@@s051 Two readings that the course always uses. <b>Composing</b>: with the right weights one builds other vectors (here [3, 2]). <b>Decomposing</b>: given a vector, find the weights that produce it (slide 23 of lecture 2, "composition and decomposition of vectors"). With u = [1, 0] and v = [0, 1] every vector of the plane is obtained this way, and the weights are exactly its components.
@@s052 It is the same thing as the model
@@s053 <span class="m">w<sub>1</sub>x<sub>1</sub> + w<sub>2</sub>x<sub>2</sub></span> is a linear combination: numbers (the weights) times quantities, then summed. Learning a model means finding the weights of the right combination. And the matrix-vector product of week 3 is a linear combination of the columns of the matrix.
@@s054 3u + 2v, one piece at a time
@@s055 From 2 to many dimensions
@@s056 One of the goals of the week is "the transition from two-dimensional vector spaces to higher-dimensional spaces". The idea is reassuring: <b>nothing changes</b> in the rules. Sum, scalar multiplication, difference, length, distance and inner product are computed component by component, and with n components the formulas simply have n terms instead of 2.
@@s057 What is lost is the drawing: beyond 3 dimensions one can no longer see. That is why one reasons in 2D, where one can see, and trusts the formulas for the rest. The file of poses is the concrete example: two poses are two points of ℝ⁵⁰, and their distance is computed exactly as between two points of the plane.
@@s058 Shape and distance of <code>poses.npy</code> checked by loading the file with NumPy. The file also serves for the data collection of Assignment 1.
@@s059 Two real poses, component by component
